{"name":"Liquorice Root","hex":"#222200","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"liquorice root","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Alternative spelling of licorice root."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/liquorice_root"}],"entries":[{"title":"Liquorice","blocks":[{"text":"Liquorice or licorice is the common name of Glycyrrhiza glabra, a flowering plant of the bean family Fabaceae, from the root of which a sweet, aromatic flavouring is extracted."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dd/Illustration_Glycyrrhiza_glabra0.jpg/330px-Illustration_Glycyrrhiza_glabra0.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Liquorice","wikidata":"Q257106"},{"title":"Root","blocks":[{"text":"In vascular plants, the roots are the organs of a plant that are modified to provide anchorage for the plant and take in water and nutrients into the plant body, which allows plants to grow taller and faster. They are most often below the surface of the soil, but roots can also be aerial or aerating, that is, growing up above the ground or especially above water."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ab/Primary_and_secondary_cotton_roots.jpg/330px-Primary_and_secondary_cotton_roots.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Root","wikidata":"Q41500"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/Sections_of_liquorice_root.jpg/500px-Sections_of_liquorice_root.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/Sections_of_liquorice_root.jpg/1280px-Sections_of_liquorice_root.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/Sections_of_liquorice_root.jpg/500px-Sections